WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:06.230
Following a modestly successful release, The
Shawshank Redemption has gone on to become

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:10.240
one of the most beloved and acclaimed movies
ever made.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.580
It often sits atop all-time best lists and
boasts performances for the ages from Tim

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:22.440
Robbins as quiet, new inmate Andy Dufresne
and Morgan Freeman as wizened lifer Red.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.760
The cinematic adaptation of Stephen King's
novella is a simply told, deeply affecting

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:34.940
drama set in the imposing prison namesake,
and at its core, is a film about the impossibility

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.940
of caging the human spirit.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:42.500
As beloved as the movie is, though, there
are some secrets about Shawshank many fans

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:43.340
don't know.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.040
Framing a relationship

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.210
While many filmmakers have put Stephen King's
stories onto celluloid, the author hasn't

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.180
always been a fan of these adaptations.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:58.300
For example, he famously hated Stanley Kubrick's
version of The Shining, calling its lack of

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:04.080
character arc a "tragedy," and saying he "could
do without all the Children of the Corn sequels."

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.520
Nevertheless, he encourages new filmmakers
to adapt his work by offering young directors

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.920
the rights to his short stories for just one
dollar.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:17.460
In the early 80s, Frank Darabont paid his
buck to adapt "The Woman in the Room" and

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.880
gained some screenwriter cred by writing films
like A Nightmare on Elm Street 3 and The Blob.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.460
Bolstered by his success in the business,
Darabont approached King again, only this

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.920
time asking about the Shawshank Redemption.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.900
Evidently, King enjoyed Darabont's take on
"The Woman in the Room" so much that he sold

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.040
the Shawshank rights for a mere $1,000.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.460
However, King never even cashed the check.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:43.980
Instead, he had it framed and gave it back
to Darabont as a gift.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:48.300
Darabont has since gone on to direct other
adaptations of King's books, including The

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:53.340
Green Mile and The Mist, but he almost lost
his shot at Shawshank just as soon as he got

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:53.840
it.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.900
After writing the script for the adaptation
of Shawshank Redemption, Darabont sent the

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.900
screenplay to Castle Rock Entertainment, the
production company run by Rob Reiner.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.430
Reiner had successfully directed two Stephen
King stories before -- Stand By Me and Misery

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:12.580
-- and loved the script so much that he offered
Darabont $3 million to take it off his hands.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.740
After all, Darabont had only helmed one actual
move at this point, a made-for-TV endeavor

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:23.460
called Buried Alive, so Castle Rock also promised
to finance any film of Darabont's choice.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.420
However, the young director wouldn't change
his mind, taking a lesser sum and the directorial

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:33.850
duties that went along with it to create one
of cinema's most cherished movies and a long-lived

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:35.680
career behind the lens as well.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.400
Taking the lead

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.860
Though he wouldn't be directing the movie,
Rob Reiner did have a hand in the casting,

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:45.260
offering the role of Andy to none other than
Tom Cruise.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:49.060
The two had worked together in 1992's Oscar-nominated
A Few Good Men.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.260
While the superstar was definitely intrigued
by the project, he didn't want to work with

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:58.340
an inexperienced director like Darabont, even
though he'd later work with him in 2004's

00:02:58.340 --> 00:02:58.840
Collateral.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:03.920
After Cruise passed, the role was offered
to other celebrated stars such as Tom Hanks,

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.880
Kevin Costner, and Nicolas Cage, all of whom
said no.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:11.800
Eventually, the part made its way to Tim Robbins,
and now, it's pretty much impossible to see

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.440
anyone else in the movie.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:17.040
That wasn't the only case of casting twists
in Shawshank.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:23.200
In Stephen King's original novella, the character
of Red was an Irish-American guy with, appropriately

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:24.520
enough, reddish-gray hair.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:29.280
So when thinking about who to cast in the
part, Darabont considered two of his favorite

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.800
older, white actors: Gene Hackman and Robert
Duvall.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:38.400
However, neither actor could accept the part,
so that's when Castle Rock producer Liz Glotzer

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.200
suggested going with Morgan Freeman.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:45.580
Darabont loved the idea, and Freeman signed
on, eventually earning an Oscar nomination

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:46.540
for Best Actor.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:51.920
And sure, his hair wasn't red, but Darabont
got around that by writing a joke to explain

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:52.420
the nickname.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:54.960
"Why do the call you that?"

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.280
"Maybe it's because I'm Irish."

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.140
If you didn't get that joke before, now you
do.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:06.260
"hehehhehehehehehehhehe"

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:08.740
What's in a name?

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.500
When it comes to long titles, The Shawshank
Redemption is a bit of a mouthful.

00:04:13.849 --> 00:04:16.100
"Why you calling the thing the Shawshank Redemption?

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:17.120
Nobody can say that."

00:04:17.509 --> 00:04:22.699
Of course, the name could've been a whole
lot longer, as King's novella was called Rita

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:24.640
Hayworth and the Shawshank Redemption.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.800
Darabont didn't change it for linguistic reasons.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:32.400
Instead, he altered the name because he didn't
want to mislead casting agents—or actresses—into

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.720
thinking it was a bio-pic on '40s movie star
Rita Hayworth.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:41.460
Hayworth's main "role" in the film is as a
poster covering up Andy's secret tunnel out

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:46.180
of prison, but early on in the production
process, Darabont received a slew of resumes

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.669
and headshots from actresses interesting in
playing the "title role," as well as an agent

00:04:50.669 --> 00:04:53.320
who represented a supermodel trying to get
into acting.

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:58.120
He later said in the film's commentary track,
"That told us how carefully that agent had

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:02.260
read the script and how much hot air floats
around Hollywood from time to time."

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:04.540
And it also inspired the title change.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:06.860
Hitting it big … later

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.880
These days, pretty much everyone loves The
Shawshank Redemption, but it wasn't a huge

00:05:11.889 --> 00:05:14.069
box office success when it first came out.

00:05:14.069 --> 00:05:19.360
The movie earned just $18 million in late
1994, and then another $10 million when it

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:25.860
was re-released into movie theaters in early
1995 after landing seven Academy Award nominations.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:31.100
Fortunately for film fans everywhere, the
movie finally became a hit on home video.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:37.440
Upon its initial release on VHS, Warner Brothers
manufactured and shipped 320,000 copies of

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:39.860
The Shawshank Redemption to video stores.

00:05:40.189 --> 00:05:44.600
Despite competition like The Lion King and
Forrest Gump, The Shawshank Redemption became

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:50.869
the most rented video of 1995, eventually
grossing about $80 million in rentals, and

00:05:50.869 --> 00:05:54.460
has lived on as an unshakable piece of Hollywood
nostalgia.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:55.900
Saying thanks

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:00.900
At the end of The Shawshank Redemption—after
Andy and Red reunite—there's still one last

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:01.500
moving moment.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:05.140
It's a dedication that reads, "In memory of
Allen Greene."

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.560
So who is this guy exactly?

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.560
Well, on the director's commentary track on
the Shawshank DVD, director Frank Darabont

00:06:12.569 --> 00:06:15.699
explained that Greene was his first-ever agent.

00:06:15.699 --> 00:06:18.379
As Darabont explained, in the '80s, he was

00:06:18.379 --> 00:06:23.300
"...a set dresser on low-budget movies who
wanted to be a writer, and it's very hard

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.999
to get an agent to believe in you on that
level."

00:06:25.999 --> 00:06:30.169
But when Darabont met Greene, he discovered
a friend and an ally.

00:06:30.169 --> 00:06:35.180
Now sadly, Greene died of complications from
AIDS right before Shawshank started filming,

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:38.120
and that's why the director decided to pay
his friend tribute.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:38.880
He said,

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:44.439
"I wanted to acknowledge not just his significance
to my career but also that he was an incredibly

00:06:44.439 --> 00:06:49.379
decent, much-loved, and much-missed person
in the lives of those who knew him."

00:06:49.379 --> 00:06:53.809
So, while Allen Greene had exactly nothing
to do with the actual movie, there's a reason

00:06:53.809 --> 00:06:56.720
his name is so prominent in the dedication
line.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:58.159
Thanks for watching!

00:06:58.159 --> 00:07:00.279
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:00.279 --> 00:07:03.189
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
